{
  "analysis": "The reviewer loves the game and is making a request: they want account/cloud saving added so progress and purchases survive deletion. The request itself (R12) is not codable. But embedded in the request is a description of a currently existing design behavior: progress and in-game purchases are stored only locally, so deleting the app forfeits everything the player has accumulated. The reviewer explicitly states that this investment — money already spent and work already done — is what keeps them from deleting the game ('I really don't want that to happen'). That is accumulated investment raising the cost of exit: the player holds value (purchases, progress) that would be destroyed by leaving, and the span shows that value operating on their choice to keep the game installed. This matches the Invested (Endowed) Value indicator of Easy to Get, Hard to Lose: exit entails forfeiting value the player already holds, and the reviewer expresses reluctance to abandon those gains. No other mechanic is described: no purchase prompt, no timer, no social or chance mechanic. The conditional future willingness to spend ('I will be looking forward to be putting money in this game') describes no existing mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I know if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value (low-level indicator)",
      "rationale": "The reviewer's accumulated investment (money spent on in-game purchases plus time/work) is what prevents them from deleting the game; exit would forfeit value they already hold, and the span shows that investment operating on their choice to keep playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

