{
  "analysis": "The review has two layers. The main body describes a monetisation mechanic: after the reviewer started purchasing Poké Balls, captures allegedly began failing more often ('they keep breaking out'), burning through the purchased consumables faster and forcing repeat purchases. This carries two claims: (1) continued play/progress (capturing Pokémon, the core loop) requires buying more balls, and (2) the game secretly manipulates capture outcomes against paying players to accelerate consumption — a concealed-outcome-manipulation allegation, which the reviewer reinforces with 'I feel taken advantage of'. The money here buys items directly, not an in-game currency, so Intermediate Currency does not apply. The second layer is the edit: the reviewer claims the review itself was written and posted under their account without their authorship, and denies the social content attributed to them ('I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE'). Under the Destination of the message rule, a store review written under the player's account counts as the game acting through the player's identity. The impersonation claim does not suppress the in-game mechanics described in the text, which remain codable (per the worked example where the same situation assigned both the impersonation label and the in-game monetary labels).",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / definition",
      "rationale": "Capturing Pokémon is the game's core progression; the span states the player must keep purchasing consumable balls to continue it, making continued play contingent on payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts the game secretly worsens capture outcomes once the player starts paying, i.e., the determination of results is concealed and manipulated to drive purchases, not left to the player's skill or visible odds."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "3 stars editing this Review I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer reports a store review published under their account that they did not write, including fabricated social activity attributed to them — content published as the player, which the boundary rule counts as impersonation by the product."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two things. First, progress is extremely slow: hard levels take a week to pass. Second, tasks are described as impossible unless the player buys boosters, which frames payment as the route past obstacles that the free path cannot clear. That is the classic Pay to Progress structure: progress contingent on, or eased by, spending. The slow-progress statement alone, without any named repetitive activity (no replaying, farming, or task cycle described), does not lift to Grinding under the boundary rule, which matches the codebook counterexample almost word for word ('so hard to win that it takes too long to make any progress without spending money'). No currency is named or converted from real money, so Intermediate Currency does not apply. No timers, schedules, daily mechanics, ads, social mechanics, or chance mechanics are described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Skip indicators; vs Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "Tasks are stated to be impossible on the free path and passable by purchasing boosters, making payment the means of progression past artificial barriers."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer praises Bingo Blitz and explicitly mentions in-app purchases, contrasting this game's pricing with other games. The key mechanical description is: 'If your spend $20 on some item you get less then what your actually hoping for, but on here bingo gives you twice the number of your purchase.' This describes spending real money ($20) and receiving a quantity of something in return — in a bingo game this is the purchase of in-game credits/coins, i.e., real money converted into in-game currency, with a bonus on the conversion. Under the 'Purchases denominated in currency' boundary rule, where the currency is the object acquired and real money is the consideration, the conversion is in the span. This satisfies Intermediate Currency regardless of the reviewer's approval (R7, R8). The mention of 'very different modes an events to get into' names modes and events but describes no schedule, expiry, pressure, or mechanic attached to them, so it does not support Playing by Appointment or FOMO. No timers, waits, ads, grinding, social mechanics, or chance-based purchases are described. No search is needed: the purchase mechanic is clear from the text.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "If your spend $20 on some item you get less then what your actually hoping for, but on here bingo gives you twice the number of your purchase",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency [low-level]; 'Purchases denominated in currency' boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending real money ($20) and receiving a quantity of in-game value (bingo credits) in return, i.e., real money converted into in-game currency, with the game awarding a bonus on the exchange."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event in MONOPOLY GO!: they were three steps from a 525-dice reward with a stated 2-day window, ran out of dice, slept, and found the event replaced on next login, forfeiting the nearly-complete reward. This is a game-set schedule with an expiry that voids progress, which is the core of Playing by Appointment. FOMO's boundary rule says to code Playing by Appointment where the span describes the schedule or the loss, and FOMO only where anticipatory pressure is described; here the span narrates the clock and the loss, not anxiety driving continued play. The reviewer explicitly says they never purchased anything, so no monetary labels apply: no money enters the dice economy, no paywall is described. Waste Aversion does not fit because nothing held is stranded by pack pricing or capacity; the loss is an expired event window. Easy to Get, Hard to Lose does not fit because the endowed progress is described as already forfeited and the player is disengaging, per its counterexample rule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed",
      "rule_applied": "definition; boundary rule vs Fear of missing out (span describes the schedule and the loss, not anticipatory pressure)",
      "rationale": "A time-limited event on the game's clock expired while the player slept, wiping near-complete progress toward the reward; the player must play within the window the game sets or forfeit it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes several mechanics of MONOPOLY GO!. First, an invite-reward system: they invited friends and got them to join Facebook expecting rewards, which is the Social Pyramid Scheme pattern; the complaint is that the rewards stopped, but the mechanic of being rewarded for recruiting new players is described as the game's design (mirroring the codebook's own Forced Fellowship worked example where an invited friend was not credited). Second, the dice rolls: they saved up 500+ dice, played max bet, and got 'NO LUCK' with no shut downs or bank heists — this describes chance-determined outcomes, but no real money stake is mentioned anywhere, so this is variable reward delivery (Reward Mania), not Gambling; nor is it Illusion of Control since no rigging or concealed mechanism is alleged, just an unlucky run. Third, 'Stickers are duplicates all the way' describes a randomised pool of collectible items yielding duplicates, which implies the player is working toward completing sticker sets (the codebook's own Monopoly GO example establishes stickers are collected to complete albums) — this carries both Reward Mania (unwanted duplicate drops from a randomised pool) and Complete the Collection (the collection goal the duplicates frustrate). The inability to log in due to an internet connection error is attributed to a technical fault, and no design penalty (lost streak, forfeited reward) is described, so R11 suppresses any label there. 'Struggling to get dices' names scarcity but describes no wait/regeneration schedule and no purchase, so it codes nothing on its own. No search is needed; all terms (dice, stickers, shut downs, bank heists) are standard MONOPOLY GO! elements and the codebook itself documents the sticker album system.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "Suddenly I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme [low-level]",
      "rationale": "The game rewards the player for pulling new people (Facebook friends) into the game; the complaint that rewards stopped confirms the recruitment-for-reward mechanic is the design."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists. Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards [low-level]; Reward Mania vs Gambling boundary rule",
      "rationale": "Unpredictable outcomes of dice rolls and duplicate drops from a randomised sticker pool describe chance-based reward delivery with no monetary stake described, so Reward Mania alone, not Gambling."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Stickers in MONOPOLY GO! are collected to complete albums; the complaint about duplicates implies the player is working toward set completion and the duplicates frustrate that goal."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a deliberate interface behavior in 8 Ball Pool: when the player opens the game or enters the 1v1 section, the UI automatically scrolls to and pre-selects the highest-stakes coin table the player's balance can cover. The reviewer attributes a purpose to this design: inducing accidental entry into high-stakes matches so players lose their accumulated coins and must replenish them by buying coins with real money. This is not an outcome-rigging claim (the 'rigged' language refers to the interface, not match results), so Illusion of Control does not apply. Three mechanics are present: (1) the interface directing the player toward a specific option — the auto-scroll/auto-select of the highest table is attention-and-selection steering by the UI; (2) a default option that removes deliberation and confirmation, making accidental loss of holdings easy and funneling toward a real-money purchase — the accidental-purchase/default-to-purchase mechanism; (3) the explicit conversion of real money into the game's currency, since 'forced to buy real ones' describes buying coins with real money. The Mandatory Marathon worked example (accidental high-stakes match entry) is not matched here because no exit-forfeit is described; the loss mechanism here is the mis-click itself, not a blocked exit.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins.",
      "rule_applied": "definition / vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically steers the player's selection toward the highest-stakes option, and the span ties this direction to a monetary outcome (losing coins that must then be repurchased), satisfying the attention-direction arm with the required purchase bearing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones.",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases [low-level] / Low Barrier: Default To Purchase [low-level]",
      "rationale": "The default selection of the highest bet table removes confirmation and deliberation, making accidental loss easy with the stated purpose of driving players into real-money purchases — an accidental-purchase mechanism via a default option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "definition / vs Purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "'Forced to buy real ones' describes real money being converted into the game's coin currency (the currency is the object acquired), establishing the real-money-to-virtual-currency exchange."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

